# The anatomy of the venous system

# Venous pathophysiology

- **Normal venous return** - venous return requires <u>overcoming of venous pressure in a foot vein</u> on standing, which is equivalent to a column of blood extending from heart to the foot (e.g. approximately 100 mg), which requires:
  - <u>Auxillary muscle pump</u> - primarily from the calf muscle, with contributions from the thigh and foot muscles, **causing rise in venous pressure in deep venous system** (200-300 mmHg) during contraction to drive blood proximally
  - <u>Normal functioning of valves</u> - ensures one way directional of blood flow 1) proximally-to-distally, and 2) from the superficial system to the deep system
  - <u>Patency of deep veins</u> - patency necessary for venous blood to drain from superficial to deep system
- **Defective venous return** - defects in patency of deep veins (i.e. obstruction) or valvular function (i.e. reflux) reduces normal venous return, and an <u>ambulatory venous hypertension</u> (normally ~ 30 mmHg) which leads to clinical presentation of chronic venous insufficiency
- **Ambulatory venous hypertension** - central pathophysiological change causing Sx:
  - <u>Telengiectasia, Reticular Veins and Varicose Veins</u> - a direct result of superficial venous dilatation due to ambulatory venous hypertension
  - <u>Oedema</u> - increased venous hydrostatic pressure, resulting in alterations in Starling's force
  - <u>Venous staining</u> - extravastation of RBC, which is subsequently broken down and deposited as haemosiderin
  - <u>Pruritis, lipodermatosclerosis and venous ulcerations</u> - extravastation of proteins leading to local inflammation

# Chronic venous insufficiency and varicose veins

- **Definition of chronic venous insufficiency** - a wide spectrum of long-standing venous abnormalities that arises from ambulatory venous hypertension
- **Definition of vericose veins** - dilated, usually tortuous subcutaneous veins \>= 3mm in diameter measured in standing position with demonstrable reflux
- **Classification of chronic venous insufficiency** - CEAP (clinical - etiology - anatomy - pathophysiology) classification: 
![](img/ceap_classification_for_chronic_venous_disorders.png)
- **Sx of chronic venous insufficiency** - patients are classified into symptomatic and asymptomatic:
  - **Aching or heaviness** - Sx caused by varicose veins, which is related to the duration of dependency
    - <u>Site</u> - ver the affected vein, skin discolouration, or areas of lipodermatosclerosis or ulcerations and does not radiate
    - <u>Exacerbating factors</u> - prolonged standing or sitting in dependent position
    - <u>Relieving factors</u> - walking, elevation or compression stockings
  - **Ankle swelling** - due to oedema over ankle, calf or thigh after prolonged standing, and improves with leg elevation and walking and signifies increased severity of disease (i.e. C3 disease)
  - **Pruritis** - itching due to protein extravasculation and low grade inflammation
  - **Features of complications** - e.g. bleeding, superficial thrombophlebitis, eczema, lipodermatosclerosis and ulcerations:
- **Signs of chronic venous insufficiency** - based on CEAP category:
  - **Dilated veins** (C1) - <u>telangectasia</u> or <u>reticular veins</u> (dubious significance and are purely a cosmetic problem):
    - <u>Telangectasia</u> - confluence of intradermal venules \> 1mm in dimeter
    - <u>Reticular veins</u> - dilated, subdurnal venules 1-3mm in diameter
  - **Varicose veins** (C2) - dilated, usually tortuous subcutaneous veins \>= 3mm in diameter measured in standing position with demonstrable reflux
  - **Oedema** (C3) - dependent pitting oedema, a result of increased volume of fluid in skin and subcutaneous tissue classically increasing throughout the day and is relieved by elevation
  - **Skin changes** (C4):
    - <u>Pigmentation/Eczema</u> (C4a):
      - Pigmentation - brown discolouration due to haemosiderin deposition of the skin most frequently affecting the gaiter area
      - Eczema - erythematous dermatitis, which may progress to blistering, weeping or scaling eruptions
    - <u>Lipodermatosclerosis</u> (C4b) - a fibrosing panniculitis of the subcutaneous tissue (chronic inflammation and fibrosis) most commonly over the medial area, with firm area of induration that is tender to touch (appears as a champange bottle)
    - <u>Corona phlebectatica</u> (C4c) - ankle flares reflected by crown-shaped dilated intradermal veins on medial or lateral malleolus
  - **Venous ulcers** - healed (C5), active (C6), recurrent (C6r):
    - <u>Site</u> - usually over medial malleolus over a perforating vein, or along the course of greater or lesser saphenous distribution (can be more proximal if precipitated by trauma)
    - <u>Morphology</u> - irregular (but determined), shallow ulcers, that are extremely tender, exudative and have granultion when infected
- **Ix of chronic venous insufficiency:**
  - **Toruniquet test** - of historical or examination interests
  - **Duplex ultrasound** - gold standard prior to any inventions:
    - <u>Setting</u> - assessment at dependent position in order to standardise measurements of venous diameter and reflux
    - <u>Procedure</u>:
      - 1\. Trannsverse view to identify the longer saphenous vein and common femoral vein medial to common femoray artery ('Micky Mouse sign'), where saphenofemoral junction (SFJ) competence is assessed
      - 2\. Assessment of full lengther of greater saphenous vein for competence and reflux (at SFJ, midthigh, AK, BK, mid-calf, ankle)
      - 3\. Longitudinal view for assessment of reflux in the CFV, SFV, SFJ on colour doppler
      - 4\. Assessment of presence and competence of thigh and calf perforators
      - 5\. Transverse and longitudinal view of the lesser saphenous distribution
    - <u>Assessment</u> - assessment of superficial, deep and perforating veins for competence, reflux, and diameter
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx** - Mx dependent of CEAP classification and presence of complications:
    - <u>Asymptomatic C1-2 disease</u> - simple reassurence
    - <u>Complicated C2 disease, symptomatic disease or severe CVI (C3-6 disease)</u> - vascular referal and managed by conservative, or surgical interventions
  - **Conservative Mx** - to reduce venous pressure and to avoid complications:
    - **Leg elevation** - reduce venous pressure by promiting venous return
    - **Graduated compression stockings** - classically below knee stockings as no evidence relating length of compression and outcomes (convenient and more acceptable):
      - <u>MOA</u> - generates pressure gradient by greater compression in foot than in knee to promote venous return
      - <u>Classification</u> - British classification based on ankle pressure: 
      ![](img/british_classification_compression_stockings.png)
      - <u>Clinical efficacy</u> improves varicose vein symptoms but no effects on progression of disease
      - <u>Precautions</u>:
        - \- r/o chronic arteriay insufficiency as it may exacerbate disease
        - Inappropriate downing (e.g. rolling down) can alter pressure gradient and have serious consequence (e.g. pressure necrosis, tourniquet effects)
    - **General skin care**
    - **Ulcer care**
  - **Principles of surgical Mx** - classification based on approach, or outcomes:
    - <u>Classification based on approach</u>:
      - **Minimaly invasive surgery** - e.g. injection sclerotherapy, VenaSeal (Adhesive glue)
      - **Endovascular surgery** - e.g. Endovenous laser ablation, Endovenous radiofrequency ablation
      - **Open surgery** - e.g. Ligation, Stripping, Stab avulsions
    - <u>Classification based on outcomes</u>:
      - **Treatment of truncal disease** (disease-modifying) - e.g. ligation, stripping, and endovascular eblation
      - **Treatment of local varicosities** (cosmesis) - e.g. stab avulsion, injection sclerotherapy
  - **Ultrasound-guided foam sclerotherapy:**
  - **Endovascular interventions:**
    - **Endovenous laser ablation** (EVLA):
      - <u>Indications</u> - demonstrated success for Tx of varicose veins due to junctional or truncal incomptence (incompetency in the long, short, anterior saphenous veins, perforators, and varicosities themselves)
      - <u>Procedure</u> - ultrasound guided cannulation of the incompetent truncal vein, and subsequent thermal ablation of the vein
      - <u>Role of LA</u>:
        - 1\. Improve patient comfort during procedure
        - 2\. External compression of vein to increase contact area between vein wall and laser fibre
        - 3\. Protects adjacent structure by causing hydrodissection and acting as a heat sink
      - <u>C/I</u>:
        - Patient factor - Unsuitable for LA, e.g. needle phobia
        - Vein factor - excess tortuosity, thrmobophlebitis
    - **Endovenous radiofrequency ablation** (RFA) - similar to EVLA but differ in method of ablation
    - **Complications of endovascular interventions:**
      - Pain and bruising
      - Thromboembolic complications (\< 1%)
      - Phlebitis (\< 5%)
      - Skin burns (\< 2%)
  - **Open surgery** - Ligation +/- Stripping +/- Stab avulsions:
    - **Ligation** - tying a ligature at the point of junctional incompetence
    - **Stripping** - remove the refluxing trunk and dilated tributaries
    - **Stab avlusions** (phlebectomy) - small stab incisions and removal of axial vein reflux or varicosities
    - **Complications of standard varicose vein surgery:**
      - <u>General complications</u> - haematoma formation, wound infections (most common complication)
      - <u>Specific complications</u>:
        - **Nerve injury** - most serious complication:
          - Saphenous nerve neuropraxia - occurs in LSV stripping (7%) resulting in pain and numbness over medial aspects of calf
          - Sural nerve neuropraxia - occurs in SSV (20%) stripping resulting in pain and numbness over posterolateral aspect of calf and foot
        - **Superficial thrombophlebitis** - occurs in residual varicosities
        - **Thromboembolic complications** (\< 0.5%)

